We confirm that our development team strictly follows secure coding practices to ensure the robustness and security of the application, complying with industry standards such as OWASP Top 10 and internal secure coding guidelines wherever applicable.

The following secure coding practices are implemented across the application:
	•	Input Validation & Sanitization (Backend – Spring Boot):
All user inputs are validated and sanitized on the server-side using Bean Validation (@NotNull, @Pattern, @Size) and custom validators to prevent SQL Injection, XSS, and command injection attacks.
	•	Parameterized Queries / ORM Usage:
Database access is performed using Spring Data JPA / Hibernate with parameterized queries and repository methods, avoiding dynamic SQL string concatenation.
	•	Secure Authentication & Session Management:
Authentication and authorization are enforced using Spring Security with role-based access control (RBAC). Sessions use secure cookies with HttpOnly, Secure, and SameSite attributes and proper timeout configuration.
	•	Data Protection:
All communication is secured over HTTPS (TLS 1.2/1.3). Sensitive data is encrypted at rest using database-level encryption and application-level encryption where applicable.
	•	Secure Error Handling & Logging:
Generic error messages are returned to end users. Detailed exception information is logged securely using Logback/SLF4J for internal troubleshooting.
	•	Principle of Least Privilege:
Application service accounts and database users are configured with minimum required permissions.
	•	Dependency & Vulnerability Management:
Backend dependencies are scanned using OWASP Dependency-Check and frontend packages using npm audit. Vulnerabilities are reviewed and remediated as per severity.
	•	Security Testing & Code Quality:
Static Application Security Testing (SAST) and code quality analysis are performed using SonarQube as part of CI/CD, along with mandatory peer code reviews.



